{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/919444-gargoyle\u2019s-vigilance","id":919444,"title":"Gargoyle\u2019s Vigilance","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-06-22T19:01:40-07:00","id":919444,"title":"Gargoyle\u2019s Vigilance","content":"The Gargoyle's Vigilance devotion states:<br /> <br /> <br /> &quot;With a point of Willpower, this Devotion may be used while the vampire slumbers.<br /> <br /> An advanced version of this Devotion exists, utilizing Protean 5, costing 4 Experiences. This version allows the vampire to become a statue, immune to fire and sunlight while remaining stationary.&quot;<br /> <br /> <br /> I have a couple of questions: <ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Does that advanced version allows to become a statue indefinitely while in torpor?</li> <li>How long does the effects of the devotion (normal and advanced) last?</li> <li>What happens if you break a piece of the statue? (And for that matter how much durability it ought to have?)</li> </ol><br /> <br /> <br /> Thanks,","signature":"","edited":{"userid":4967,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","user":"lbeaumanior","date":". 06-24-2016, 07:02 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-25T16:45:28-07:00","id":921224,"title":"","content":"1 - It doesn't say anything about changing the duration of the normal devotion.<br /> 2 - it lasts for the length of time the vampire remains stationary or until the vampire enters Torpor (unless a willpower is spent).<br /> 3 - all being a statue does is render the vampire immune to fire and sunlight, it doesn't make them any tougher otherwise. So the same thing that happens if you break off a piece of a vampire normally, I'd guess.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When one is accustomed to privilege, equality seems like oppression.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7195-tidygamer","name":"TidyGamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7195_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"644"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-25T18:28:57-07:00","id":921271,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TidyGamer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/919444-gargoyle\u2019s-vigilance?p=921224#post921224\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1 - It doesn't say anything about changing the duration of the normal devotion.<br /> 2 - it lasts for the length of time the vampire remains stationary or until the vampire enters Torpor (unless a willpower is spent).</div> </div> </div> </div>The second one is important because if the advanced one is used, you could enter torpor as a statue, if an Elder knows this devotion can he/she do an Enkil and Akasha (From Anne Rice Vampire stories) and slumber as a statue for basically forever?<br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4967-lbeaumanior","name":"lbeaumanior","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4967_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1351"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-25T20:33:28-07:00","id":921321,"title":"","content":"Sure, there's nothing really wrong with that and the vampire chronicles has always been a WoD vampire inspiration.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>When one is accustomed to privilege, equality seems like oppression.</i>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7195-tidygamer","name":"TidyGamer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7195_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Aug 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"644"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-06-26T06:35:58-07:00","id":921465,"title":"","content":"Well, it's not like it's overpowered or anything. A stone statue can easily be broken with the right tools or if you make it fall on the ground (the smaller parts like arms and stuff will break). Honestly, I'd rule it so the stone is tougher than normal stone and more resistant to blunt damage. Otherwise, you could get a pretty stupid situation where after a vampire goes sleeping and becomes a standing statue, there's an earthquake and the vampire falls over and their head breaks off, causing final death.<br /> <br /> That devotion seems more like a thing that seems cool but actually is more troubke than it's worth to me. At least, especially for a long torpor, it would require some pretty heavy preparation. Assuming the clothes turn to stone as well (and why not), I'm not sure finding a stone statue of a dude in a hoodie in foetal position is much less suspicious than the normal torpid mummified version which could pass as a dead homeless guy.<br /> <br /> It's just pretty hard to make this power be really useful. People would notice a new gargoyle/statue in a Cathedral. You'd have to dominate everyone that takes care of the place so that they don't think twice about it and pick a place that isn't so well known that people will tell the difference just by consulting a book about it. You'd have to be wearing old clothes or be naked because hyperrealist stone statues are not a thing that people really do anymore. You'd have to pass as some ancient work of art or a reproduction and be in a place where such an object would be expected to be. It's good if you own an estate with a garden and statues in it. When you go into torpor, you just remove one of the statues that looks like you and sit in its place. Now, granted, that could make for some cool tricks (you wanna get out of town for good, go falsify some museum records and be shipped to a foreign museum as a work of art, then wake up there and feed on the security guard that makes their round at night), but would need some good preparation.<br /> <br /> Also, stone is actually pretty restrictive. It's not a material that's been used everywhere. Europe had very realistic statues in ancient Greece and Rome, and the Renaissance until Bronze kind of took over, so that would work. Pretty much all along the Silk Road, there was a stone sculpture practice that rivaled (and influenced/was influenced by) the Greeks' but also a lot of bronze too. Japan is just as much, if not more, about wooden sculpture, as is most of Africa (and African sculpture, with a few exceptions, is generally not intended to be realistic so there's another problem). Egypt had sculpture obviously (from which stemmed the greek sculpture), but life sized statues aren't that common and it's pretty stylized. Mezoamerican and South-American civilizations made stone sculptures, but they are heavily stylized as well and finding a realistic statue in an archeological site would be groundbreaking.<br /> <br /> So you gotta worry about what you're gonna look like as a statue, what country you're gonna do it in, and take all that into account to anticipate how people will react to finding you as a statue. Honestly, if you can afford to control all that, you can probably find a safe place to hide in, and if you have a secure hiding spot, chances are you don't need to be able to turn into stone.<br /> <br /> That said, I think it'd be pretty cool you could buy the devotion multiple times with a different material each time. Bronze would be a good choice, but some shiny steel like Jeff Koons' stuff could actually be easier if one wants to hide in a contemporary art collection. Glass, although fragile would also potentially sit well in some overly rich person's mansion.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7104-s-brechen","name":"S. Brechen","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"171"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}